The strongest name triggers are Todoist for broad recommendations, cross-platform use, and Google Calendar or Teams integrations; TickTick for calendars, reminders, widgets, Linux, and focus features; Microsoft To Do for Outlook, Teams, and Microsoft queries; and Google Tasks for Google Calendar and Google ecosystem queries. Any.do has clear calendar, reminders, and sharing associations, but is less likely to appear as a default pick. These associations are reflected in the apps’ official feature pages and current app roundups. [Todoist integrations](https://www.todoist.com/integrations), [Microsoft To Do in Microsoft 365](https://learn.microsoft.com/office365/servicedescriptions/office-365-platform-service-description/office-365-suite-features), [Google Tasks Help](https://support.google.com/tasks/answer/7675772?hl=en), [TickTick](https://ticktick.com/?language=en_us), [Any.do Help Center](https://support.any.do/en/), [TechRadar’s 2026 app roundup](https://www.techradar.com/best/best-todo-list-apps)

For the query-level pass, I treated broad “best” prompts as likely to name several recognizable apps, while device, calendar, collaboration, ADHD, and specialized-use prompts mainly trigger the apps that fit. I treated “name,” “review,” and implementation prompts as weak recommendation triggers. The top-down base-rate check yields the same ordering: Todoist appears most often across generic answers; TickTick and Microsoft To Do follow; Google Tasks rises on Google-specific prompts; Any.do is less consistently included.

